{"author":{"id":"85a3c31a64931f83958f26b24045fef8415ea6ff04d942d15661891c77fc0695"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794215239,"nonce":"Yu994JkRy3RCnmdUHJemUOudLn8=","message":"Imports across files landed in the working tree (uncommitted). russl_compiler::Program loads a root file and every file its use items reach: a directory is a module tree laid out as Rust lays out modules (dir/a/b.russl or dir/a/b/mod.russl is crate::a::b), paths start at crate, super or self, a module reached twice loads once. A missing file is an error at the use item naming both paths it looked for, relative to the tree. Diagnostics carry their file through Span::file (each parsed string is its own file to proc-macro2), and render_program shows each against its own file. russl_build::compile / compile_in / compile_with and the CLI (russl check|emit \u003cfile\u003e [--root dir] [--deny-warnings], emit adds spirv as binary) both use it. Tests: 5 in russl_build (diamond, mod.russl, missing module, error in an imported file, bare path), 3 driving the CLI binary, resolve() unit test.\n\nMissing russl module in the macro: the hidden alias is now `russl_module` and its name takes the span of the module it is looked up in, so rustc reports \"cannot find `russl_module` in `plain`\" at `plain` in the use item instead of at the whole russl! block naming `__russl_source`. use_paths keeps the spans the path was written with. Not under a test yet: trybuild compile-fail tests are still open in 8fad30d.\n\nStill open here: generics and the extension trait.","files":null}]}